@title: Reading Time — Luyện đọc hiểu tiếng Anh
@author: Cho học sinh 10-12 tuổi (trình độ A2)
@lang: en

# Cách dùng sách này

Mỗi bài gồm ba phần: **một bài đọc**, **từ mới**, và **câu hỏi**.

Cách làm đúng thứ tự:

- Đọc hết bài **một lần, không tra từ**. Chưa hiểu hết cũng không sao.
- Trả lời câu hỏi bằng cách nhìn lại bài đọc để tìm câu trả lời.
- **Sau đó** mới xem phần từ mới.
- Đọc lại bài lần hai. Lần này sẽ dễ hơn nhiều.

> Đừng tra từ ngay khi gặp từ lạ. Đoán nghĩa từ ngữ cảnh là kỹ năng quan trọng nhất khi đọc, và chỉ luyện được khi em chịu khó đoán trước.

Mỗi ngày một bài là đủ.

# 1. The Girl Who Counted Birds

Every morning before school, Linh sits by her window with a notebook.

She writes down every bird she sees. The date, the time, the kind of bird, and how many.

She started two years ago, when she was nine. Her teacher said that scientists count things, and Linh wanted to try.

At first it was boring. Some mornings she saw only two or three sparrows. She wrote them down anyway.

But after a year, something interesting happened. Linh looked back through her notebook and noticed a pattern. In March and April, she saw many more birds than in other months. And there was one kind of bird — a small yellow one — that she only saw in autumn.

She showed her notebook to her science teacher.

"This is real data," her teacher said. "Do you know what this means? The yellow birds are migrating. They pass through here twice a year."

Linh had discovered something true, all by herself, with nothing but a notebook and two years of patience.

Now her whole class counts birds. There are twenty-six notebooks instead of one.

Their teacher sends the numbers to a university every month.

## New words

@vocab
notebook | /ˈnəʊtbʊk/ | quyển vở | She writes in her notebook every day.
sparrow | /ˈspærəʊ/ | chim sẻ | Only two sparrows came today.
pattern | /ˈpætən/ | quy luật, hình mẫu lặp lại | She noticed a pattern in the numbers.
data | /ˈdeɪtə/ | dữ liệu | This is real data.
migrate | /maɪˈɡreɪt/ | di cư | The birds migrate twice a year.
patience | /ˈpeɪʃns/ | sự kiên nhẫn | It took two years of patience.
discover | /dɪˈskʌvə/ | khám phá ra | She discovered something true.
@end

## Questions

- How old was Linh when she started counting birds?
- What did she notice after one year?
- Why does the yellow bird only appear in autumn?
- How many notebooks does the class have now?
- What two things did Linh need to make her discovery?

# 2. The Boy and the Broken Clock

Minh's grandfather had an old clock on the wall. It had stopped working long before Minh was born.

"Why don't you fix it?" Minh asked one day.

"Nobody can fix it," his grandfather said. "It is eighty years old."

Minh was eleven. He decided that "nobody can" was not the same as "it cannot be done."

He took the clock down very carefully and put it on the kitchen table. Then he took a photograph of it before he opened it — so he would remember where everything went.

Inside there were forty-three small pieces. He drew each one in a notebook and numbered them.

It took him three weeks. Most of that time he was not fixing anything. He was watching videos, reading, and learning what each piece was called.

In the end, the problem was very small. One tiny spring had come loose. It took eleven seconds to put back.

When the clock started ticking again, his grandfather did not say anything for a long time.

Then he said: "Your grandmother wound that clock every Sunday for fifty years."

## New words

@vocab
fix | /fɪks/ | sửa | Nobody can fix it.
carefully | /ˈkeəfəli/ | cẩn thận | He took it down carefully.
photograph | /ˈfəʊtəɡrɑːf/ | bức ảnh | He took a photograph first.
piece | /piːs/ | mảnh, bộ phận | There were forty-three pieces.
spring | /sprɪŋ/ | lò xo | One tiny spring had come loose.
loose | /luːs/ | lỏng, rời ra | The spring had come loose.
tick | /tɪk/ | kêu tích tắc | The clock started ticking.
wind | /waɪnd/ | lên dây cót | She wound the clock every Sunday.
@end

## Questions

- Why did Minh take a photograph before opening the clock?
- How long did the repair take? How long did the *actual* fix take?
- What was Minh doing for most of the three weeks?
- Why do you think his grandfather did not speak at first?

# 3. The Longest Journey

Some animals travel further than you can imagine.

The Arctic tern is a small white bird. It weighs about one hundred grams — less than a bar of soap.

Every year it flies from the Arctic in the north all the way to Antarctica in the south. Then it flies back.

That is about seventy thousand kilometres a year.

An Arctic tern can live for thirty years. In its lifetime, it flies far enough to reach the moon and come back — three times.

Scientists learned this only recently. They put tiny trackers, lighter than a paperclip, on the legs of a few birds. A year later, they caught the same birds again and read the data.

The birds did not fly in a straight line. They followed the wind, making huge curves across the ocean, because flying with the wind uses much less energy than flying against it.

Nobody taught them this. The first time a young tern makes the journey, it already knows the way.

## New words

@vocab
Arctic | /ˈɑːktɪk/ | Bắc Cực | It lives in the Arctic.
weigh | /weɪ/ | nặng | It weighs one hundred grams.
lifetime | /ˈlaɪftaɪm/ | cả đời | In its lifetime it flies very far.
tracker | /ˈtrækə/ | thiết bị theo dõi | They used tiny trackers.
paperclip | /ˈpeɪpəklɪp/ | cái kẹp giấy | Lighter than a paperclip.
straight | /streɪt/ | thẳng | They did not fly in a straight line.
curve | /kɜːv/ | đường cong | They made huge curves.
energy | /ˈenədʒi/ | năng lượng | It uses less energy.
@end

## Questions

- How heavy is an Arctic tern?
- How far does it fly in one year?
- Why do the birds fly in curves instead of straight lines?
- How did scientists find out about the journey?
- What is surprising about a young tern's first journey?

# 4. The Shop with No Prices

In a small town in Japan, there is a café where the food has no fixed price.

You eat, and then you pay whatever you think it was worth.

When the owner first opened it, everyone told her the same thing: *people will cheat you*.

That is not what happened.

Most people paid about the normal price. Some paid less, usually students or old people with little money. And some paid much more — one man once paid five times the normal price, because, he said, he had eaten there for free when he was young and poor.

After three years, the café was still open.

The owner says the reason is simple. "When you trust people," she says, "most of them try to deserve it."

But she also admits something important: this works in a small town where people come back again and again. In a busy railway station, where nobody comes twice, it might not work at all.

## New words

@vocab
fixed | /fɪkst/ | cố định | The food has no fixed price.
worth | /wɜːθ/ | đáng giá | Pay what you think it was worth.
cheat | /tʃiːt/ | gian lận | People will cheat you.
owner | /ˈəʊnə/ | chủ | The owner opened it three years ago.
trust | /trʌst/ | tin tưởng | When you trust people...
deserve | /dɪˈzɜːv/ | xứng đáng | Most try to deserve it.
admit | /ədˈmɪt/ | thừa nhận | She admits something important.
@end

## Questions

- How do customers decide how much to pay?
- What did people warn the owner about? Was it true?
- Why did one man pay five times the price?
- Why might this idea *not* work in a railway station?
- Do you think this would work in your town? Why or why not?

# 5. The Museum That Was Almost Lost

In 1972, a small museum in England was closing down. Nobody visited it any more.

The town council decided to throw away most of the objects inside. They were old farm tools, broken pots, and boxes of things nobody had looked at for years.

A young woman named Sarah worked there part time. She asked for two weeks to sort through the boxes before they were thrown out.

She was given one week.

On the fourth day, in a box marked *"miscellaneous — probably rubbish"*, she found a small metal disc, about the size of a coin, black with dirt.

She cleaned it very slowly with a soft brush.

It was Roman. Nearly two thousand years old.

She kept looking. In the same box she found eleven more.

The coins turned out to be from a period that historians knew very little about. Today they are in the British Museum, and researchers still study them.

The little museum did not close. It is still open now.

Sarah later said the strangest part was the label on the box. Somebody, many years earlier, had looked inside, decided it was rubbish, and written it down — and after that, nobody looked again.

## New words

@vocab
council | /ˈkaʊnsl/ | hội đồng thị trấn | The town council decided.
object | /ˈɒbdʒɪkt/ | đồ vật | Old objects filled the boxes.
sort through | /sɔːt θruː/ | lục soát, phân loại | She sorted through the boxes.
miscellaneous | /ˌmɪsəˈleɪniəs/ | linh tinh, hỗn tạp | The box said "miscellaneous".
rubbish | /ˈrʌbɪʃ/ | rác, đồ bỏ | Probably rubbish.
disc | /dɪsk/ | vật hình đĩa | A small metal disc.
historian | /hɪˈstɔːriən/ | nhà sử học | Historians knew very little.
label | /ˈleɪbl/ | nhãn dán | The label said it was rubbish.
@end

## Questions

- How much time did Sarah ask for? How much was she given?
- Where did she find the first coin?
- Why were the coins important to historians?
- What did Sarah find strangest about the whole story?
- What lesson does the label on the box teach us?

# 6. Two Brothers and One Bicycle

Nam and Duc were brothers. Nam was twelve and Duc was nine.

They had one bicycle between them, and they fought about it constantly.

Their mother got tired of the fighting. One evening she said: "Tomorrow I will decide. One of you will keep the bicycle. The other will get nothing."

Both boys went to bed feeling worried.

In the morning, before their mother woke up, Nam went to find his brother.

"I have an idea," he said. "I will use it in the morning to go to school. You use it in the afternoon to visit your friends. And on Sunday we take turns — one week you, one week me."

"Why are you telling me this?" Duc asked. "You are older. Mother will give it to you."

"Maybe," Nam said. "But then you get nothing. And I do not want that."

When their mother came out, both boys explained the plan together.

She listened, and then she smiled.

"I was never going to take it away," she said. "I only wanted to see whether you could solve it yourselves."

## New words

@vocab
constantly | /ˈkɒnstəntli/ | liên tục | They fought constantly.
tired of | /ˈtaɪəd ɒv/ | chán ngán | She got tired of the fighting.
worried | /ˈwʌrid/ | lo lắng | Both boys felt worried.
take turns | /teɪk tɜːnz/ | thay phiên nhau | We take turns on Sunday.
solve | /sɒlv/ | giải quyết | Could they solve it themselves?
explain | /ɪkˈspleɪn/ | giải thích | They explained the plan together.
@end

## Questions

- What was the problem between the brothers?
- Who suggested the solution, and why is that surprising?
- What did the mother really want?
- Do you think her method was fair? What else could she have done?

# 7. The Language That Came Back

On the island of Man, between England and Ireland, people once spoke a language called Manx.

In 1974, the last person who had grown up speaking it died. His name was Ned Maddrell. He was ninety-seven.

Scientists wrote that Manx was now a **dead language**.

But something unusual happened.

Before Ned died, researchers had recorded his voice. They had hours of him speaking, telling stories, counting, singing.

A small group of people on the island began to study those recordings. They learned the sounds. They taught each other. They wrote books for children.

In 2001, a school opened where all lessons were taught in Manx.

Today there are children on that island whose first language is Manx — a language that was officially declared dead before they were born.

In 2009, the United Nations changed its records. Manx is no longer listed as extinct. It is now listed as **revived**.

## New words

@vocab
island | /ˈaɪlənd/ | hòn đảo | The island of Man.
grow up | /ɡrəʊ ʌp/ | lớn lên | He grew up speaking Manx.
record | /rɪˈkɔːd/ | ghi âm | They recorded his voice.
researcher | /rɪˈsɜːtʃə/ | nhà nghiên cứu | Researchers kept the tapes.
lesson | /ˈlesn/ | bài học | All lessons were in Manx.
declare | /dɪˈkleə/ | tuyên bố | It was declared dead.
extinct | /ɪkˈstɪŋkt/ | tuyệt chủng | No longer listed as extinct.
revive | /rɪˈvaɪv/ | hồi sinh | The language was revived.
@end

## Questions

- When did the last native speaker die?
- What made it possible to bring the language back?
- How long was it between his death and the school opening?
- Why do you think people worked so hard to save this language?

# 8. The Experiment You Can Do Tonight

Here is something you can try before you go to bed.

You need three glasses of water. One very cold, one warm, and one at room temperature.

Put your left hand in the cold water and your right hand in the warm water. Wait for one minute. Try to keep them still.

Now put **both** hands into the middle glass at the same time.

Something strange happens.

Your left hand tells you the water is warm. Your right hand tells you the same water is cold.

Both hands are in exactly the same water.

This is not a trick. Your skin does not measure temperature. It measures **change** in temperature. Your left hand feels the water getting warmer. Your right hand feels it getting cooler.

Scientists call this a *relative* measurement. It tells you how things compare, not what they really are.

Many things work this way. A room feels quiet after a loud street. A small gift feels big after a bad week.

Your senses are very good at noticing change, and quite bad at knowing the truth.

## New words

@vocab
room temperature | /ruːm ˈtemprətʃə/ | nhiệt độ phòng | One glass at room temperature.
still | /stɪl/ | yên, không cử động | Keep them still.
at the same time | /ət ðə seɪm taɪm/ | cùng lúc | Put both hands in at the same time.
skin | /skɪn/ | da | Your skin does not measure temperature.
measure | /ˈmeʒə/ | đo | It measures change.
compare | /kəmˈpeə/ | so sánh | How things compare.
senses | /ˈsensɪz/ | các giác quan | Your senses notice change.
@end

## Questions

- What do you need for this experiment?
- What does each hand tell you at the end? Why do they disagree?
- What does your skin actually measure?
- The last two sentences are not about water. What are they about?
